qudt:CountingUnit foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:CountingUnit rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:CountingUnit rdfs:comment "Used for all units that express counts. Examples are Atomic Number, Number, Number per Year, Percent and Sample per Second."^^<http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#HTML> .
qudt:CountingUnit rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:CountingUnit rdfs:label "Counting Unit"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:CountingUnit rdfs:subClassOf qudt:DimensionlessUnit .
qudt:CurrencyUnit foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:CurrencyUnit rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:CurrencyUnit rdfs:comment "Currency Units have their own subclass of unit because: (a) they have additonal properites such as 'country' and (b) their URIs do not conform to the same rules as other units."^^<http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#HTML> .
qudt:CurrencyUnit rdfs:comment "Used for all units that express currency."^^<http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#HTML> .
qudt:CurrencyUnit rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:CurrencyUnit rdfs:label "Currency Unit"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:CurrencyUnit rdfs:subClassOf qudt:DimensionlessUnit .
qudt:DataEncoding foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:DataEncoding rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:DataEncoding rdfs:comment "<p><em>Data Encoding</em> expresses the properties that specify how data is represented at the bit and byte level. These properties are applicable to describing raw data.</p>"^^<http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#HTML> .
qudt:DataEncoding rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:DataEncoding rdfs:label "Data Encoding"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:DataEncoding rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Aspect .
qudt:Datatype foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:Datatype <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "A data type is a definition of a set of values (for example, \"all integers between 0 and 10\"), and the allowable operations on those values; the meaning of the data; and the way values of that type can be stored. Some types are primitive - built-in to the language, with no visible internal structure - e.g. Boolean; others are composite - constructed from one or more other types (of either kind) - e.g. lists, arrays, structures, unions. Object-oriented programming extends this with classes which encapsulate both the structure of a type and the operations that can be performed on it. Some languages provide strong typing, others allow implicit type conversion and/or explicit type conversion."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:Datatype qudt:informativeReference "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_type"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI> .
qudt:Datatype qudt:informativeReference "http://foldoc.org/data+type"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI> .
qudt:Datatype qudt:informativeReference "http://www.princeton.edu/~achaney/tmve/wiki100k/docs/Data_type.html"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI> .
qudt:Datatype rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:Datatype rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:Datatype rdfs:label "QUDT Datatype"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:Datatype rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Concept .
qudt:DateTimeStringEncodingType foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:DateTimeStringEncodingType <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "Date Time encodings are logical encodings for expressing date/time quantities as strings by applying unambiguous formatting and parsing rules."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:DateTimeStringEncodingType rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:DateTimeStringEncodingType rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:DateTimeStringEncodingType rdfs:label "Date Time String Encoding Type"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:DateTimeStringEncodingType rdfs:subClassOf qudt:StringEncodingType .
qudt:DecimalPrefix foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:DecimalPrefix rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:DecimalPrefix rdfs:comment "A <em>Decimal Prefix</em> is a prefix for multiples of units that are powers of 10."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:DecimalPrefix rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:DecimalPrefix rdfs:label "Decimal Prefix"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:DecimalPrefix rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Prefix .
qudt:DerivedUnit foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:DerivedUnit qudt:dbpediaMatch "http://dbpedia.org/resource/Category:SI_derived_units"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI> .
qudt:DerivedUnit rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:DerivedUnit rdfs:comment "A DerivedUnit is a type specification for units that are derived from other units."^^<http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#HTML> .
qudt:DerivedUnit rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:DerivedUnit rdfs:label "Derived Unit"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:DerivedUnit rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Unit .
qudt:DimensionlessUnit foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:DimensionlessUnit rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:DimensionlessUnit rdfs:comment "A Dimensionless Unit is a quantity for which all the exponents of the factors corresponding to the base quantities in its quantity dimension are zero."^^<http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#HTML> .
qudt:DimensionlessUnit rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:DimensionlessUnit rdfs:label "Dimensionless Unit"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:DimensionlessUnit rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Unit .
qudt:Discipline foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:Discipline rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:Discipline rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:Discipline rdfs:label "Discipline"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:Discipline rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Concept .
qudt:DoublePrecisionEncoding foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:DoublePrecisionEncoding qudt:bytes "64"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
qudt:DoublePrecisionEncoding rdf:type qudt:FloatingPointEncodingType .
qudt:DoublePrecisionEncoding rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:DoublePrecisionEncoding rdfs:label "Single Precision Real Encoding"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:Encoding foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:Encoding <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "An encoding is a rule or algorithm that is used to convert data from a native, or unspecified form into a specific form that satisfies the encoding rules. Examples of encodings include character encodings, such as UTF-8."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:Encoding rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:Encoding rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:Encoding rdfs:label "Encoding"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:Encoding rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Concept .
qudt:EndianType foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:EndianType qudt:informativeReference "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endianness"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI> .
qudt:EndianType qudt:plainTextDescription "In computing, endianness is the ordering used to represent some kind of data as a sequence of smaller units. Typical cases are the order in which integer values are stored as bytes in computer memory (relative to a given memory addressing scheme) and the transmission order over a network or other medium. When specifically talking about bytes, endianness is also referred to simply as byte order.  Most computer processors simply store integers as sequences of bytes, so that, conceptually, the encoded value can be obtained by simple concatenation. For an 'n-byte' integer value this allows 'n!' (n factorial) possible representations (one for each byte permutation). The two most common of them are: increasing numeric significance with increasing memory addresses, known as little-endian, and its opposite, called big-endian."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:EndianType rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:EndianType rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:EndianType rdfs:label "Endian Type"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:EndianType rdfs:subClassOf qudt:EnumeratedValue .
qudt:EnumeratedQuantity foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:EnumeratedQuantity rdf:type qudt:AspectClass .
qudt:EnumeratedQuantity rdfs:label "Enumerated Quantity"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:EnumeratedQuantity rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Concept .
qudt:EnumeratedValue foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:EnumeratedValue <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "<p>This class is for all enumerated and/or coded values.  For example, it contains the dimension objects that are the basis elements in some abstract vector space associated with a quantity kind system. Another use is for the base dimensions for quantity systems. Each quantity kind system that defines a base set has a corresponding ordered enumeration whose elements are the dimension objects for the base quantity kinds. The order of the dimensions in the enumeration determines the canonical order of the basis elements in the corresponding abstract vector space.</p>\n\n<p>An enumeration is a set of literals from which a single value is selected. Each literal can have a tag as an integer within a standard encoding appropriate to the range of integer values. Consistency of enumeration types will allow them, and the enumerated values, to be referred to unambiguously either through symbolic name or encoding. Enumerated values are also controlled vocabularies and as such need to be standardized. Without this consistency enumeration literals can be stated differently and result in  data conflicts and misinterpretations.</p>\n\n<p>The tags are a set of positive whole numbers, not necessarily contiguous and having no numerical significance, each corresponding to the associated literal identifier. An order attribute can also be given on the enumeration elements. An enumeration can itself be a member of an enumeration. This allows enumerations to be enumerated in a selection. Enumerations are also subclasses of Scalar Datatype. This allows them to be used as the reference of a datatype specification.</p>"^^<http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#HTML> .
qudt:EnumeratedValue qudt:informativeReference "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enumeration"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI> .
qudt:EnumeratedValue rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:EnumeratedValue rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:EnumeratedValue rdfs:label "Enumerated Value"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:EnumeratedValue rdfs:subClassOf <http://www.linkedmodel.org/schema/dtype#EnumeratedValue> .
qudt:EnumeratedValue rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Concept .
qudt:EnumeratedValue rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Verifiable .
qudt:Enumeration foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:Enumeration qudt:dbpediaMatch "http://dbpedia.org/resource/Enumeration"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI> .
qudt:Enumeration qudt:informativeReference "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enumerated_type"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI> .
qudt:Enumeration qudt:informativeReference "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enumeration"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI> .
qudt:Enumeration rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:Enumeration rdfs:comment "<p>An enumeration is a set of literals from which a single value is selected. Each literal can have a tag as an integer within a standard encoding appropriate to the range of integer values. Consistency of enumeration types will allow them, and the enumerated values, to be referred to unambiguously either through symbolic name or encoding. Enumerated values are also controlled vocabularies and as such need to be standardized. Without this consistency enumeration literals can be stated differently and result in  data conflicts and misinterpretations.</p>\n\n<p>The tags are a set of positive whole numbers, not necessarily contiguous and having no numerical significance, each corresponding to the associated literal identifier. An order attribute can also be given on the enumeration elements. An enumeration can itself be a member of an enumeration. This allows enumerations to be enumerated in a selection. Enumerations are also subclasses of <em>Scalar Datatype</em>. This allows them to be used as the reference of a datatype specification.</p>"^^<http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#HTML> .
qudt:Enumeration rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:Enumeration rdfs:label "Enumeration"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:Enumeration rdfs:subClassOf <http://www.linkedmodel.org/schema/dtype#Enumeration> .
qudt:Enumeration rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Concept .
qudt:EnumerationScale foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:EnumerationScale rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:EnumerationScale rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:EnumerationScale rdfs:label "Enumeration scale"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:EnumerationScale rdfs:subClassOf <http://www.linkedmodel.org/schema/dtype#Enumeration> .
qudt:EnumerationScale rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Scale .
