qudt:QuantityType foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:QuantityType <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "\n  A $\\textit{Quantity Type}$ is an enumeration of quantity kinds. \n  It specializes $\\boxed{dtype:EnumeratedValue}$ by constrinaing $\\boxed{dtype:value}$ to instances of $\\boxed{qudt:QuantityKind}$.\n  "^^<http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/LatexString> .
qudt:QuantityType rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:QuantityType rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:QuantityType rdfs:label "Quantity type"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:QuantityType rdfs:subClassOf qudt:EnumeratedValue .
qudt:QuantityValue foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:QuantityValue rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:QuantityValue rdfs:comment "A <i>Quantity Value</i> expresses the magnitude and kind of a quantity and is given by the product of a numerical value <code>n</code> and a unit of measure <code>U</code>. The number multiplying the unit is referred to as the numerical value of the quantity expressed in that unit. Refer to <a href=\"http://physics.nist.gov/Pubs/SP811/sec07.html\">NIST SP 811 section 7</a> for more on quantity values."^^<http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#HTML> .
qudt:QuantityValue rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:QuantityValue rdfs:label "Quantity value"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:QuantityValue rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Concept .
qudt:QuantityValue rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Quantifiable .
qudt:RatioScale foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:RatioScale qudt:informativeReference "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Level_of_measurement"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI> .
qudt:RatioScale rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:RatioScale rdfs:comment "The ratio type takes its name from the fact that measurement is the estimation of the ratio between a magnitude of a continuous quantity and a unit magnitude of the same kind (Michell, 1997, 1999). A ratio scale possesses a meaningful (unique and non-arbitrary) zero value. Most measurement in the physical sciences and engineering is done on ratio scales. Examples include mass, length, duration, plane angle, energy and electric charge. In contrast to interval scales, ratios are now meaningful because having a non-arbitrary zero point makes it meaningful to say, for example, that one object has \"twice the length\" of another (= is \"twice as long\"). Very informally, many ratio scales can be described as specifying \"how much\" of something (i.e. an amount or magnitude) or \"how many\" (a count). The Kelvin temperature scale is a ratio scale because it has a unique, non-arbitrary zero point called absolute zero."^^<http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#HTML> .
qudt:RatioScale rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:RatioScale rdfs:label "Ratio scale"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:RatioScale rdfs:seeAlso qudt:IntervalScale .
qudt:RatioScale rdfs:seeAlso qudt:NominalScale .
qudt:RatioScale rdfs:seeAlso qudt:OrdinalScale .
qudt:RatioScale rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Scale .
qudt:Rule foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:Rule rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:Rule rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:Rule rdfs:label "Rule"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:Rule rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Concept .
qudt:Rule rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Verifiable .
qudt:RuleType foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:RuleType rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:RuleType rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:RuleType rdfs:label "Rule Type"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:RuleType rdfs:subClassOf qudt:EnumeratedValue .
qudt:ScalarDatatype foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:ScalarDatatype <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "Scalar data types are those that have a single value. The permissible values are defined over a domain that may be integers, float, character or boolean. Often a scalar data type is referred to as a primitive data type."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:ScalarDatatype rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:ScalarDatatype rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:ScalarDatatype rdfs:label "Scalar Datatype"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:ScalarDatatype rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Datatype .
qudt:Scale foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:Scale rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:Scale rdfs:comment "Scales (also called \"scales of measurement\" or \"levels of measurement\")  are expressions that typically refer to the theory of scale types."^^<http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#HTML> .
qudt:Scale rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:Scale rdfs:label "Scale"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:Scale rdfs:subClassOf qudt:Concept .
qudt:ScaleType foundation:lastUpdatedAt "2026-03-04T04:10:29Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
qudt:ScaleType qudt:plainTextDescription "Scales, or scales of measurement (or categorization) provide ways of quantifying measurements, values and other enumerated values according to a normative frame of reference.  Four different types of scales are typically used. These are interval, nominal, ordinal and ratio scales."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:ScaleType rdf:type owl:Class .
qudt:ScaleType rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt> .
qudt:ScaleType rdfs:label "Scale type"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
qudt:ScaleType rdfs:subClassOf qudt:EnumeratedValue .
